---
name: float-literal-suffix
description: "0d / 2.5D / 1.5f / 3F lex as numbers, not as a number followed by an identifier"
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# A trailing `d` / `D` / `f` / `F` belongs to the literal

`BUGS.md` `v2-front-drops-float-literal-suffix`. Both self-hosted fronts stripped a trailing `L`/`l`
Long suffix — each with a comment explaining that without it the `L` lexed as a separate identifier
and the program died with `unbound global: L`. The Double/Float suffixes never got the same
treatment, so `val x: Double = 0d` failed with **`unbound global: d`** on the default front and on
the legacy one alike.

Not only a syntax gap: it took **three of 33 bench-corpus workloads out of the v2 matrix** as "v2
cannot run this", because the generated wrapper declared its Double sink as `0d`. The programs
themselves always ran.

```scalascript
val a: Double = 0d
val b: Double = 2.5d
val c: Double = 1.5f
val e: Double = 3D
val g: Long = 10L
val h: Double = 0.5
val i: Double = 1.0e2
val j: Double = 1e2
println(a)
println(b)
println(c)
println(e)
println(g)
println(h)
println(i)
println(j)
```

`10L` and the plain `0.5` are the guard that the existing paths were not disturbed: the Long suffix
had always worked, and a suffix-less float must not start consuming the letter after it. The two
exponent forms cover the third emission point in the lexer — `1.0e2` reaches it through the
dot-float branch and `1e2` through the exponent branch, and a fix wired into only one of them leaves
the other dropping the suffix.
